#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/scripts

# The whole site, over TLS. This is run_website.etcs with ONE line added and
# the port changed, and that is the entire claim the TLS work is making: a
# site that was written before TLS existed is served over it without any
# part of the site being told.
#
# Diff against run_website.etcs is exactly:
#   - SetPort 8080  ->  SetPort 8443
#   + EnableTLS ...
#
# Everything downstream is untouched. The landing page, the file tree, both
# lobbies, and both of their detached web traces are byte-identical to the
# plaintext deployment.
#
# Cert first (see tls_server.etcs for why -addext is not optional):
#   openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -days 30 \
#     -keyout /tmp/etcs-key.pem -out /tmp/etcs-cert.pem \
#     -subj "/CN=localhost" \
#     -addext "subjectAltName=DNS:localhost,IP:127.0.0.1"
#
# Order matters twice over, exactly as it does in run_website.etcs.
#
# The LANDING PAGE is added first. HttpServer::ResolvePath walks typed
# children in the order they were added and takes the first match, and
# chess_web.etcs's own board_page also answers "/" -- so whichever is
# attached first owns it. Adding landing here, before either lobby is
# detached, is what makes that deterministic rather than a function of which
# trace ran first.
#
# The lobbies are detached AFTER Start. Each one spawns `web` by that same
# name, and spawn_entity retargets a pending name already bound to a live
# entity of the same module and tag -- PersistentNames is process-scope, not
# per-script -- so they find THIS server instead of minting a second one.
# Their own Start calls are no-ops: StartConcrete returns false when a
# manager already exists.
#
# THAT IS ALSO WHAT MAKES THEM INHERIT TLS. They re-bind to a gate that is
# already terminating, so their routes are served encrypted without
# chess_web.etcs or forum_web.etcs containing a single TLS-aware line. Two
# entire subsystems, neither of which knows what a handshake is, reached
# over HTTPS because the termination sits below the layer they plug into.
# If this file is the only one that had to change, the layering is right.

NetworkProvider
HttpServer web
spawn web
SetPort 8443

# BEFORE Start. Start mints the ConnectionManager and Opens it in one step,
# and Open begins accepting immediately, so there is no later point at which
# the gate exists but has not yet taken a connection. Stores paths only;
# Start is what loads them, and Start REFUSES to come up if either file
# fails rather than silently serving this site in plaintext on 8443.
EnableTLS /tmp/etcs-cert.pem /tmp/etcs-key.pem

web.add(StaticHtmlPage landing)
SetHtmlFromFile ./www/index.html
context NetworkProvider::HttpServer web

# Everything else is disk layout as url layout: /chess.html, /forum.html,
# /whitepaper.html. One page type, no per-document entity, and adding a
# document is copying a file rather than editing this trace.
web.add(FileHtmlPage tree)
LoadFromDisk ./www
ListPaths tree
context NetworkProvider::HttpServer web

# The tar-pit: a self-registering, gate-level filter that catches
# bot-scan-shaped paths (.env, wp-admin, .php, ...) and answers them with a
# deliberately slow 404 instead of routing them anywhere real -- see
# TarpitNode.h for why it lives here as its own entity rather than a flag on
# HttpServer or ConnectionManager. Byte-identical to run_website.etcs's own
# copy of this block -- the tar-pit does not know it is being served over
# TLS any more than chess or forum do; termination happens below every
# subscriber, filtered ones included.
#
# FULLY QUALIFIED, and it has to be -- see run_website.etcs's own copy of
# this block for what a bare `TarpitNode tarpit` silently does instead when
# a tag is already in context (it spawns a second HttpServer under that
# name and every @tarpit below resolves to it).
context NetworkProvider::TarpitNode tarpit
spawn tarpit
LoadDefaultTarpitPatterns

# AddHandler @tarpit Request @tarpit Filter registers the tar-pit as a
# FILTERED gate-level consumer, tried before unfiltered ones. AddHandler
# @web Serve is NOT redundant with Start's own implicit default -- that
# default only fires when handlers_ is EMPTY, and adding the tar-pit above
# means it no longer is. Without this second line the tar-pit would be the
# only registered consumer and every real request would find no fallback.
context NetworkProvider::HttpServer web
AddHandler @tarpit Request @tarpit Filter
AddHandler @web Serve

Start
IsStarted

detach chess_lobby.etcs
detach forum_lobby.etcs

# https://localhost:8443/
#
# Self-signed, so the browser will warn -- that is the cert, not the
# transport. Accept it and the site behaves exactly as the plaintext one.
#
# EXPECTED IN THE LOG: chess_web.etcs carries its own `SetPort 8080`, and
# against this server it is REFUSED with a visible line --
#
#   SetPort: already started on port 8443 -- REFUSING to retarget to 8080.
#
# That is the correct outcome, not a failure of this trace. A started
# server's port is not writable: the listener is bound once inside Open and
# nothing re-reads it, so accepting the value would leave the entity
# reporting 8080 while serving 8443, and a later Stop/Start would come up on
# the wrong port. The refusal is the bug being caught rather than a bug.
#
# You will NOT see it under run_website.etcs, because there the value being
# set matches the port already in use, and re-declaring what is already true
# is a no-op rather than a retarget.
